class=bgc-single-article><div
class=bgc-single-body>
<a
class=bgc-single-title href=https://openmetal.io/resources/blog/how-to-size-a-private-cloud-cluster-before-you-sign-a-contract/ ><h4 class="widget-title">How to Size a Private Cloud Cluster Before You Sign a Contract</h4>		</a><p><span>Aug 28, 2026</span></p><div
class=bgc-single-excerpt><p>We walk through a real worked example for sizing a private cloud cluster before committing to a contract, covering how to translate your VM count into node count, why redundancy and replication overhead eat into your raw numbers, and where network bandwidth becomes the limiting factor as a cluster grows.</p></div></div></div><div
class=bgc-single-article><div
class=bgc-single-body>
<a
class=bgc-single-title href=https://openmetal.io/resources/blog/terraform-vs-ansible-for-openstack-which-one-do-you-actually-need/ ><h4 class="widget-title">Terraform vs Ansible for OpenStack: Which One Do You Actually Need?</h4>		</a><p><span>Aug 26, 2026</span></p><div
class=bgc-single-excerpt><p>We break down what Terraform and Ansible each actually do differently, why the overlap between them confuses newcomers, and a practical framework for deciding which one to reach for, or whether you need both, when automating a private cloud deployment.</p></div></div></div><div
class=bgc-single-article><div
class=bgc-single-body>
<a
class=bgc-single-title href=https://openmetal.io/resources/blog/comparing-openmetal-hetzner-and-ovhcloud-for-proxmox-ve-hosting/ ><h4 class="widget-title">Comparing OpenMetal, Hetzner, and OVHcloud for Proxmox VE Hosting</h4>		</a><p><span>Aug 10, 2026</span></p><div
class=bgc-single-excerpt><p>We compare three dedicated server providers commonly considered for Proxmox VE hosting, OpenMetal, Hetzner, and OVHcloud, across real hardware specs, current pricing, storage architecture, and support model, so you can match the provider to your actual workload rather than just the sticker price.</p></div></div></div><div
class=bgc-single-article><div
class=bgc-single-body>
<a
class=bgc-single-title href=https://openmetal.io/resources/blog/intel-tdx-openstack-confidential-vms/ ><h4 class="widget-title">Intel TDX on OpenMetal: Bare Metal Today, OpenStack Orchestration Next</h4>		</a><p><span>Aug 06, 2026</span></p><div
class=bgc-single-excerpt><p>Intel TDX confidential VMs run on OpenMetal dedicated bare metal hardware today, available on on XL v5, with OpenStack Nova orchestration slated for after Hibiscus.</p></div></div></div><div
class=bgc-single-article><div
class=bgc-single-body>
<a
class=bgc-single-title href=https://openmetal.io/resources/blog/migrating-off-azure-entra-id-and-cosmos-db-are-the-hard-part/ ><h4 class="widget-title">Migrating Off Azure: Entra ID and Cosmos DB Are the Hard Part</h4>		</a><p><span>Jul 17, 2026</span></p><div
class=bgc-single-excerpt><p>We look at why Azure&#8217;s egress fees are no longer the sharpest lock-in mechanism, and walk through the specific managed services (Azure Functions, Cosmos DB, Service Bus, Logic Apps, Azure AD B2C / Entra External ID) that actually make leaving Azure hard, including the one place Azure is more open than either AWS or GCP, and the one place it&#8217;s arguably worse.</p></div></div></div><div
class=bgc-single-article><div
class=bgc-single-body>
<a
class=bgc-single-title href=https://openmetal.io/resources/blog/google-clouds-real-lock-in-lives-in-spanner-and-firestore-not-egress-fees/ ><h4 class="widget-title">Google Cloud&#8217;s Real Lock-In Lives in Spanner and Firestore, Not Egress Fees</h4>		</a><p><span>Jul 15, 2026</span></p><div
class=bgc-single-excerpt><p>We look at why Google Cloud&#8217;s egress fees are no longer the sharpest lock-in mechanism, and walk through the specific managed services (Cloud Functions, Firestore, Cloud Spanner, Pub/Sub, Cloud Workflows, Identity Platform) that actually make leaving Google Cloud hard, including where GCP&#8217;s lock-in profile is genuinely different from AWS&#8217;s.</p></div></div></div><div
class=bgc-single-article><div
class=bgc-single-body>
<a
class=bgc-single-title href=https://openmetal.io/resources/blog/the-real-aws-lock-in-is-managed-services-not-egress/ ><h4 class="widget-title">The Real AWS Lock-In Is Managed Services, Not Egress</h4>		</a><p><span>Jul 13, 2026</span></p><div
class=bgc-single-excerpt><p>We look at why AWS egress fees are no longer the lock-in mechanism people think they are, and walk through the specific managed services (Lambda, DynamoDB, Step Functions, EventBridge, SQS/SNS, Cognito, API Gateway) that actually make leaving AWS hard.</p></div></div></div><div
class=bgc-single-article><div
class=bgc-single-body>
<a
class=bgc-single-title href=https://openmetal.io/resources/blog/how-to-prevent-private-cloud-migration-delays/ ><h4 class="widget-title">How to Prevent Private Cloud Migration Delays</h4>		</a><p><span>Jul 08, 2026</span></p><div
class=bgc-single-excerpt><p>Planning a private cloud project? Organizing well from the start can prevent expensive and time-consuming delays. Our guide explains why hosted private cloud projects stall across migration and day two operations and shows how to prevent delays with better planning, architecture, ownership, and operational readiness.</p></div></div></div><div
class=bgc-single-article><div
class=bgc-single-body>
<a
class=bgc-single-title href=https://openmetal.io/resources/blog/top-8-reasons-companies-leave-public-cloud-in-2026/ ><h4 class="widget-title">Top 8 Reasons Companies Leave Public Cloud in 2026</h4>		</a><p><span>Jul 06, 2026</span></p><div
class=bgc-single-excerpt><p>A skimmable breakdown of the main business and technical drivers pushing companies from public cloud to hosted private cloud, covering cost control, compliance, performance, and operational control.</p></div></div></div><div
class=bgc-single-article><div
class=bgc-single-body>
<a
class=bgc-single-title href=https://openmetal.io/resources/blog/what-hipaa-requires-from-the-infrastructure-running-your-healthcare-ai-workloads/ ><h4 class="widget-title">What HIPAA Requires from the Infrastructure Running Your Healthcare AI Workloads</h4>		</a><p><span>Jul 02, 2026</span></p><div
class=bgc-single-excerpt><p>Healthcare AI workloads carry the same HIPAA obligations as any system touching PHI. This article covers what the 2026 Security Rule update requires from AI infrastructure, why vector embeddings count as PHI, and how dedicated private cloud simplifies the compliance documentation burden.</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div
